{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mass.CoordinationSurfaceDeficit","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.perNucleon_surfaceExponent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.perNucleon_surfaceExponent","kind":"theorem","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L181-L187","doc":"Feeding the `d`-dimensional surface fraction into `perNucleon_split` gives exactly\n`perNucleonExp` with `p = 1/d`: **the surface exponent is the inverse dimension.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.surfaceFraction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.surfaceFraction","kind":"def","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L177-L179","doc":"The `d`-dimensional surface fraction, as a hypothesis-level definition: a compact\n`d`-dimensional cluster has boundary of dimension `d − 1`, so `A_surf/A = c·A^(−1/d)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_ne_semf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_ne_semf","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L167-L175","doc":"**The chain cannot be the SEMF surface law.** The `d = 1` chain law `ε − ε/A` and any\n`d ≠ 1` law with the same amplitude differ at some `A > 0`. In particular the SEMF's `p = 1/3`\n(a three-dimensional cluster) is not reachable by the production word's path graph, whatever\n`ε` is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.perNucleonExp_exponent_inj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.perNucleonExp_exponent_inj","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L152-L165","doc":"**Distinct exponents are distinct laws.** If two laws with the same nonzero amplitude agree\nat every `A > 0`, their exponents are equal. So the exponent is not free: measuring it measures\n`1/d`, and no choice of the other constants can disguise a wrong dimension. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_eq_perNucleonExp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_eq_perNucleonExp","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L146-L150","doc":"The chain is `perNucleonExp ε ε 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.perNucleonExp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.perNucleonExp","kind":"def","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L142-L144","doc":"The per-nucleon law with surface exponent `p`: `B/A = a − b·A^(−p)`. For a compact\n`d`-dimensional cluster `p = 1/d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_is_dimension_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_is_dimension_one","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L133-L138","doc":"The chain's law is the `d = 1` member of the family `B/A = a − b·A^(−1/d)`: taking\n`a = ε`, `b = ε` and `1/d = 1` reproduces it exactly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_perNucleon","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_perNucleon","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L127-L131","doc":"**The chain's per-nucleon binding is `ε − ε/A`:** exponent `p = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_bondCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SurfaceExponentIsDimension.chain_bondCount","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SurfaceExponentIsDimension.lean#L121-L125","doc":"**The path graph's bond count is `A − 1`.** With interior degree `k = 2`, endpoint degree\n`k' = 1` and exactly two endpoints, the handshake count of `CoordinationSurfaceDeficit` gives\n`A − 1` — which is the production word's one inter-nucleon letter per adjacent pair. ","signatureChunk":0}]}